  You're in luck.  I just started working on an early game survival guide.


  "The main key to winning and not dying is patience."
    - The rec.games.roguelike.nethack FAQ

  Early characters are death on two legs, their OWN death.  Run away
like a coward, use Elbereth, hide behind pets, use ranged attacks, etc.
while desperately scrabbling through everything you find for ways to
improve your character, especially AC.

  Much later you'll be able to waltz through the dungeon like you owned
the place.  (Even that will just be temporary if you stop trying to
improve your character.  It also gets harder and harder to improve your
character as the game goes along.)  


  - The key rule is: Survive.  Dead characters accomplish little.

  - Read the Guidebook again.

  - Play with patience:

 "** Time out! I step away from the computer and go have a smoke so
that I don't do anything stupid, like hit any keys. After collecting
my thoughts, and confident that I will take my time, I go back... **"
   - Krysia's Krusader


  Here is a semi-guide to ttyrecs of mrivan's games on nethack.alt.org.
The first 23 games are ascensions.  There's more than one ttyrec per game.  
He made these ttyrecs specifically to help players, complete with embedded
comments:

  <http://www.mindspring.com/~jove/mrivan_nao_ttyrec_index.htm>

  Here are my suggestions.

  Tactics:

  - Bravely run away!

  - Elbereth early and often.

  - Throw rocks if you don't have any other distance attack.
     - aka "Rock it's world."

  - Improve your AC.

  - Don't forget to #pray as a last resort.

  - Every character death is suicide.  Something in its inventory would 
    have saved it.  Hence the Dread Lord YASD (Yet Another Stupid Death.)

  - Hostile monsters won't step on a square engraved with Elbereth
    that has an item on it.  Use this to block corridors.  Then
    throw rocks if you've nothing better.

  - Don't fight peacefuls.

  - Find out what weapons to use/#enhance for each class, especially
    ranged weapons.

  "Melee combat is the best way to die in NetHack.  If the monster is
in melee range so are you." - Murphy's Law of Combat (NetHack version.)

  "It is a lot more dangerous to be meleeing carelessly than using
ranged combat carelessly." - BWIGLEY

  Hwei Sheng TEOH:  "What does it take to make a low-level wizard survive?"
  Boudewijn Waijers "Retreat often. Don't ever fight hand to hand. If you 
                     cannot be hit, you don't even *need* armour."  

  - Watch the monsters.  
    - What do they do to harm you?  
    - What do they do when they're in trouble?


  - Forget enchanting, fountains, spell casting and identifying early on.

  "I'd just read some old Rob Ellwood posts on forgetting spellcasting,
identifying and enchanting until later. I tried it, and what do you
know!" - Milton Pope, YAAP

  Trying too hard to identify items too early is a deathtrap.  Monsters will
identify emergency items for you when they use them.  Harmful potions
will be identified when they throw them at you.  Wands and armor are the
main items to identify early because you can do so safely.  (See the spoilers.)

  - Do NOT #twoweapon.  Early on it's a DevTeam deathtrap.
    - Costs you a shield and the ability to hit.

  - Play on the nethack.alt.org NetHack server (aka NAO.)
    - Be on IRC channel #nethack on freenode.org.  
      - People there can watch your game on NAO and give help.


  - Don't be burdened.  I don't care how much it hurts.
     - Allocate carrying capacity to armor and weapons first.
     - Only carry a few emergency items.
     - Make stashes by the downstairs before you descend.  (So
        monsters won't get your stuff and use it against you.)
        - Your first container should melt your heart with happiness
          because you can centralize your stashes and protect them.

  - Try on every non-cursed piece of armor your find to improve your AC.
     (Your pet can help here.)
      - Especially accessory armor.  You'll see a lot of it so you're 
        more likely to find high enchantments on them:
        - Helmets
        - Boots
        - Gloves
        - Shields
      - One +4 piece of accessory can make the early game much easier.
     Focusing almost solely on improving AC was the turning point in
       my game play.

  - Buy divine protection when you can.
     - Practicing the "protection racket" can teach you to survive by
       relying on your pets and not fighting yourself.

  - You can make hostile dogs, cats, and horses peaceful by 
     throwing food at them that they can't eat:
      - tins
      - meat to horses (They'll eat food rations and be tamed though.)
      - vegetarian food to dogs and cats (lichen corpse.)
    This saves them for later taming as replacement pets.  It also means
    you don't have to fight them.

  - Hide behind your pet(s).  (This should be a separate spoiler.)
     - Let your pet kill monsters so it will grow up big and strong.
         - Two large dogs/cats can handle almost anything in the Mines.
         - This will also keep your XL lower.  That means weaker
           monsters will be generated.  Your buffed up pets will have
           an easier time handling them.
         - Once you've got a couple of good pets you can concentrate
           on exploring while they pay with their food.
         - Pets are a great way to handle nymphs, killer bees, ants, etc.
           while you're resting on a half dozen dust Elbereths.
     - Tame a spare pet. 
        - Use fortune cookies, non-poisonous corpses, even food rations.
        - NOT tripe rations, aka "Pet Velcro".  Carry a tripe ration
          in open inventory and pets stick much closer to you.
     - Pets are much more likely to move toward you if:
       - You're carrying a meatball or tripe ration in open inventory.
       - They can see you and you're less than three squares away.
       - 'a'pplying a non-cursed magic whistle teleports all you pets to you.
       - 'a'pplying a non-cursed eucalyptus leaf ditto.
       - A leash will keep your pet from moving more than 2 squares 
            away from you.  A dog/cat on a leash will whine when it's
            adjacent to both you and a trap.
       - A tin whistle will call nearby pets.  So will a non-cursed bell.
        (Not sure exactly what this means.)
       - Stethoscopes are great for monitoring pet health/level.
           
       Learning to lead a pet will be very useful leading monsters later 
          on.
     - Pets are a pain but can save you early on.  By midgame you can 
         abandon them.



  - Get a ranged attack.  You'll have to learn what works best for each
       role.

